#ifndef HG_ABOUT_TEXT_H
#define HG_ABOUT_TEXT_H
#define HG_ABOUT_README_W L"# hgfloater\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**English** | [한국어](README.ko.md)\r\n" L"\r\n" L"hgfloater is a lightweight desktop utility for **Windows 11 and above**. A small\r\n" L"translucent widget floats on your desktop; hovering it opens a dashboard that\r\n" L"launches your shortcuts, switches between running windows, and puts volume,\r\n" L"brightness, opacity, and a command console one click away. It is written in pure\r\n" L"C against the Win32 API with zero external dependencies, so it starts instantly\r\n" L"and stays out of your way.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"\r\n" L"---\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## Table of Contents\r\n" L"\r\n" L"1. [Overview](#1-overview)\r\n" L"2. [Install and First Run](#2-install-and-first-run)\r\n" L"3. [The Two Windows](#3-the-two-windows)\r\n" L"4. [The Floater](#4-the-floater)\r\n" L"5. [The Taskbox](#5-the-taskbox)\r\n" L"6. [The Toolbar](#6-the-toolbar)\r\n" L"7. [The Options Menu](#7-the-options-menu)\r\n" L"8. [The Command Box](#8-the-command-box)\r\n" L"9. [Monitor Thumbnails](#9-monitor-thumbnails)\r\n" L"10. [Notes](#10-notes)\r\n" L"11. [Keyboard Reference](#11-keyboard-reference)\r\n" L"12. [Mouse Reference](#12-mouse-reference)\r\n" L"13. [Configuration File](#13-configuration-file)\r\n" L"14. [Files and Directories](#14-files-and-directories)\r\n" L"15. [Building From Source](#15-building-from-source)\r\n" L"16. [Project Layout](#16-project-layout)\r\n" L"17. [Tests and Verification](#17-tests-and-verification)\r\n" L"18. [About the Developer](#18-about-the-developer)\r\n" L"19. [The HellGates Series](#19-the-hellgates-series)\r\n" L"20. [License](#20-license)\r\n" L"\r\n" L"---\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 1. Overview\r\n" L"\r\n" L"hgfloater aims at one thing: making everyday desktop control faster than the\r\n" L"stock taskbar allows. It works as a **quick launcher**, a **task switcher**, a\r\n" L"**system control panel** (volume, per-display brightness and scaling, window\r\n" L"opacity, screen lock), a **command box** for driving windows by name and number,\r\n" L"and a **note pad** - all reachable from a widget you can park anywhere on any\r\n" L"monitor.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Design principles worth knowing before you use it:\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Nothing runs in the background but the app itself.** No services, no\r\n" L"  installer. A single `hgfloater.exe`. It writes exactly one registry value, and\r\n" L"  only if you switch **Start with Windows** on - the ordinary per-user `Run`\r\n" L"  entry, which you can see and delete yourself.\r\n" L"- **Plain files on disk, in one folder.** Settings live in a plain `config.ini`\r\n" L"  under your user profile, and notes are plain `.txt` files beside it. Nothing\r\n" L"  is a database, everything is editable by hand, and the program writes no logs,\r\n" L"  caches, or temporary files.\r\n" L"- **Everything is adjustable in place.** Size, opacity, font size, grid shape,\r\n" L"  and colors change live with the wheel or the keyboard, and persist by\r\n" L"  themselves.\r\n" L"- **Keyboard and mouse are equal citizens.** Every action has both a pointer\r\n" L"  gesture and a key.\r\n" L"- **It follows the system theme.** Switching Windows between light and dark mode\r\n" L"  re-colors the widgets immediately.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 2. Install and First Run\r\n" L"\r\n" L"1. **Download** the latest `hgfloater.exe` from the\r\n" L"   [Releases](https://github.com/rubidus-api/hgfloater/releases/tag/v26.07.29k)\r\n" L"   page.\r\n" L"2. **Run it.** There is no installer. On first launch it creates\r\n" L"   `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\` with a `config.ini` and a `shortcuts`\r\n" L"   folder, then shows the floater.\r\n" L"3. **Add shortcuts.** Drop `.lnk` or `.url` files into\r\n" L"   `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\shortcuts`. They appear in the taskbox\r\n" L"   automatically; press `Esc` in the taskbox to re-scan the folder immediately.\r\n" L"4. **Summon it from anywhere** with `Win + Alt + Space` (configurable).\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Only one instance runs at a time. Launching `hgfloater.exe` again simply\r\n" L"signals the running copy instead of starting a second one.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 3. The Two Windows\r\n" L"\r\n" L"hgfloater is built from two windows that trade places:\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Window | What it is | How you get it |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| **floater** | A small always-on-top widget: clock, date, host name, and system bars. | The default state. |\r\n" L"| **taskbox** | The dashboard: running windows, your shortcuts, and the toolbar. | Hover or click the floater, or press the global hotkey. |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Expanding centers the taskbox on the floater and then pushes it fully inside\r\n" L"that monitor's work area, so a floater parked at a screen edge never yields a\r\n" L"clipped dashboard. Collapsing returns the floater to where it was — and if you\r\n" L"moved the taskbox while it was open, the floater travels the same distance, so\r\n" L"the pair stays where you left it.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 4. The Floater\r\n" L"\r\n" L"The floater is the resting state: a compact translucent panel that stays on top\r\n" L"of other windows.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**What it shows**\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Clock and date**, refreshed every second, sized proportionally to the\r\n" L"  widget itself.\r\n" L"- **Host name** in a thin line across the top.\r\n" L"- **Status bars** for CPU, temperature, GPU, memory, and battery: horizontal\r\n" L"  bars running behind everything - the host name included - with small labels\r\n" L"  down the left edge and each row's **reading printed down the right**. Full\r\n" L"  width means 100%; the two temperature rows use their own scale, below. A row\r\n" L"  whose value the machine does not report is absent entirely, which is why\r\n" L"  desktops have no battery row and machines without sensors have no `TMP` or\r\n" L"  `GPU`. Set `show_stats=0` in `config.ini` to hide the whole panel.\r\n" L"- **`TMP`** is drawn on a fixed **20 to 100 degree Celsius** scale rather than as\r\n" L"  a percentage, with the reading printed on its own bar, because the difference\r\n" L"  between 55 and 75 degrees matters more than a few pixels of bar length can\r\n" L"  say.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"  **`GPU`** follows it on the same scale, and it gets a better answer than the\r\n" L"  CPU does: the adapter's own sensor, read through the same interface Task\r\n" L"  Manager uses, with no vendor SDK and no driver. Plenty of drivers report\r\n" L"  nothing, and those machines simply have no GPU row.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"  The CPU reading is read from the **ACPI thermal zone** your firmware exposes. That is the\r\n" L"  only temperature reachable without installing a kernel driver and running as\r\n" L"  administrator, and it is worth knowing what it is: **a sensor on the board\r\n" L"  near the CPU, not the CPU die**. It tracks the processor without being it, and\r\n" L"  it lags under a sudden load. Many machines expose no zone at all - on those\r\n" L"  the row simply is not there, the same way the battery row is absent on a\r\n" L"  desktop. If you need true per-core readings, use a tool built around a\r\n" L"  hardware-monitoring driver; hgfloater will not become one.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**What you can do to it**\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Hover** — opens the taskbox in place. The floater hides itself while the\r\n" L"  taskbox is up.\r\n" L"- **Left click** — toggles the taskbox.\r\n" L"- **Left drag** — in **`F` adjust mode**, moves the floater anywhere on any\r\n" L"  monitor. Outside that mode the pointer arriving on the floater opens the\r\n" L"  taskbox before a drag can begin, so press `F` first.\r\n" L"- **Alt + Wheel** — opacity.\r\n" L"- **Ctrl + Wheel** or **Ctrl + Left drag** — font size, which scales the whole\r\n" L"  widget with it.\r\n" L"- **Alt + Arrows / WASD** — moves the window from the keyboard.\r\n" L"- **`T`** — opens the taskbox. **`C`** — opens the Command Box. **`F1`** — About.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"When the cursor leaves the taskbox, it collapses back to the floater after a\r\n" L"half-second grace period, so brushing past the edge does not dismiss it.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 5. The Taskbox\r\n" L"\r\n" L"The taskbox is a grid of icons with a status line on top and a toolbar of\r\n" L"built-in buttons.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### 5.1 Running windows and shortcuts\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Task icons** come first: one per visible top-level window, in the order\r\n" L"  Windows reports them.\r\n" L"- **Shortcut icons** follow: one per `.lnk` or `.url` in your shortcuts folder.\r\n" L"- **Left click** activates a task or launches a shortcut.\r\n" L"- **Left drag** on a task icon reorders it within the grid.\r\n" L"- **Right click** (or `Enter` / `F2` on the focused icon) opens its menu:\r\n" L"  - **Run (&R)** — start a new instance, or launch the shortcut.\r\n" L"  - **Focus (&F)** — switch to the existing window.\r\n" L"  - **Close Window (&X)** — task icons only.\r\n" L"  - **Open File Location (&O)** — shortcut icons only.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### 5.2 The status line\r\n" L"\r\n" L"A single-line read-only field across the top of the taskbox.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- It shows **one message at a time** — the most recent one replaces its\r\n" L"  predecessor.\r\n" L"- Ten seconds after the last message it falls back to the **current time**,\r\n" L"  written as `2026. 11. 23.(Tue) 13:24`, and refreshes as the minute changes.\r\n" L"- **Right click** it to open the options menu (the same one the `O` button\r\n" L"  shows).\r\n" L"- **Left drag** it to move the whole taskbox.\r\n" L"- **Ctrl + Wheel** over it changes only its own font size.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### 5.3 Shape and size\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Drag any border** to change the grid: the taskbox snaps to whole columns, so\r\n" L"  no half icon is ever left hanging.\r\n" L"- **Ctrl + Wheel** (or `Ctrl` + `+` / `-`) scales icons and text together and\r\n" L"  keeps the window proportional.\r\n" L"- **Alt + Wheel** (or `Alt` + `+` / `-`) changes opacity.\r\n" L"- **`Ctrl` + `R` / `0`, or `F5`** resets position, size, and opacity.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 6. The Toolbar\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Twelve built-in buttons sit in the same grid as the icons. Their order is fixed.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Button | Click | Drag / Wheel |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| **`R`** Resize | — | **Drag** resizes the taskbox grid. |\r\n" L"| **`M`** Move | **Click** moves the taskbox aside (see below). | **Drag** moves the window. |\r\n" L"| **`X`** Exit | Quits hgfloater. | — |\r\n" L"| **`D`** Desktop | Minimizes every window; click again to restore. | — |\r\n" L"| **`O`** Options | Opens the [options menu](#7-the-options-menu). | — |\r\n" L"| **`C`** Command | Opens the [Command Box](#8-the-command-box). | — |\r\n" L"| **`A`** Alpha | — | **Wheel** changes taskbox opacity. The red background brightens as opacity rises. |\r\n" L"| **`B`** Brightness | — | **Wheel** changes screen brightness in 5% steps. The green background brightens with it. |\r\n" L"| **`V`** Volume | Toggles mute. A thick accent border marks the muted state. | **Wheel** changes the volume. The blue background brightens with it. |\r\n" L"| **`F`** Floater | Collapses to the floater for tuning (see below). | — |\r\n" L"| **`P`** Pin | Pins the taskbox open. | — |\r\n" L"| **`N`** Note | Opens the [note list](#10-notes). | — |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**`M` — move aside.** Clicking the move handle without dragging nudges the pair\r\n" L"out of the way on its own, just far enough to stop covering the spot it was\r\n" L"sitting on. Clicks keep their heading, so pressing it repeatedly walks the\r\n" L"window across the screen; when that heading runs out of room it turns\r\n" L"counter-clockwise — north, west, south, east, and back to north. If no direction\r\n" L"has room, nothing moves.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**`B` — brightness.** The wheel moves brightness in **1% steps**, finer than the\r\n" L"5% opacity and volume use, because a monitor's real scale is known rather than\r\n" L"assumed.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"hgfloater tries three things per display, in order, and remembers which one\r\n" L"answered. First the **low-level DDC/CI** path: it reads the monitor's\r\n" L"capabilities string and, if that advertises the luminance control, drives it on\r\n" L"whatever scale the monitor actually reports — which is often not 0 to 100. Then\r\n" L"the **high-level** brightness call, for monitors that support that but not the\r\n" L"first. Last a **gamma ramp**, which is worth being honest about: it dims the\r\n" L"picture, not the backlight, and costs contrast.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"A monitor can refuse all of them — DDC/CI switched off in its own menu, a cable\r\n" L"or adapter that does not carry the channel, a KVM in the path, or a monitor that\r\n" L"advertises a control it does not honour. That display shows\r\n" L"**Brightness (unavailable)** rather than accepting a click that does nothing.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"A **laptop's internal panel** is not a DDC/CI device at all, so none of that\r\n" L"would reach it. It gets its own path: hgfloater recognises the built-in display\r\n" L"by its connector and drives the real backlight through Windows' own brightness\r\n" L"service, the same one the system slider uses. That moves the lamp, not the\r\n" L"picture.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**`F` — floater adjust.** Collapses the dashboard and suspends hover-to-expand,\r\n" L"so you can tune the floater with `Ctrl + Wheel` (size) and `Alt + Wheel`\r\n" L"(opacity) without the taskbox reappearing under your cursor — and **drag it**\r\n" L"to put it where you want, which is only possible here for the same reason.\r\n" L"Click the floater without dragging to go back.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**`P` — pin.** While pinned, moving the mouse away no longer collapses the\r\n" L"taskbox, and the button carries an accent border. Explicit closes — `X`, `Esc`,\r\n" L"the global hotkey, a floater click — still work. Click again to unpin.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 7. The Options Menu\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Open it with the `O` toolbar button or by right-clicking the status line.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Open Shortcuts Folder** — opens the shortcuts directory in Explorer.\r\n" L"- **Edit Configuration** — opens `config.ini` in Notepad.\r\n" L"- **Start with Windows** — checked when hgfloater launches at sign-in. Toggling\r\n" L"  it writes or removes one value under\r\n" L"  `HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run`, holding the quoted\r\n" L"  path of the running executable. No installer, no elevation, nothing else\r\n" L"  touched. Switching it on again after moving `hgfloater.exe` re-registers where\r\n" L"  the file actually is. **Reset Settings** leaves it alone.\r\n" L"- **About...** — this document, rendered inside the app.\r\n" L"- **Reset Settings** — restores default geometry, opacity, sizes, and colors.\r\n" L"- **Select Audio Device** — lists the output devices with the current one\r\n" L"  checked, and offers a **Mute** toggle.\r\n" L"- **_(one entry per display)_** — every connected monitor gets its own submenu,\r\n" L"  named the way you would name it: its number, the monitor name the driver reads\r\n" L"  out of the display's EDID, and the connector it hangs off, as in\r\n" L"  `2. DELL U2720Q (DP)`. Each submenu holds everything the app can do to that\r\n" L"  one display:\r\n" L"  - **Preview Window** — turns that display's\r\n" L"    [thumbnail](#9-monitor-thumbnails) on and off.\r\n" L"  - **Scale** — the Windows display-scaling percentages (100, 125, 150, 175,\r\n" L"    200, 225). The current scale is checked; percentages the monitor cannot\r\n" L"    reach are greyed out. Choosing one changes that monitor's scale\r\n" L"    system-wide, exactly as the Settings app would.\r\n" L"  - **Brightness** — the backlight in quarter steps (0, 25, 50, 75, 100). The\r\n" L"    step nearest the monitor's current level is checked. Displays that answer\r\n" L"    DDC/CI are driven directly; the rest fall back to a gamma curve applied to\r\n" L"    that display alone, so dimming one screen leaves the others as they were.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"  The port is the socket on the graphics adapter, not the cable: a USB-C screen\r\n" L"  running DisplayPort alt mode reports as `DP` and cannot be told apart from a\r\n" L"  DisplayPort socket. `USB-C` appears when DisplayPort is tunnelled over USB4 or\r\n" L"  Thunderbolt, which is the only case Windows reports separately.\r\n" L"- **Lock Screen (Power Off)** — locks the workstation.\r\n" L"- **Exit** — quits.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 8. The Command Box\r\n" L"\r\n" L"A standalone console window, opened with the `C` toolbar button or the `C` key\r\n" L"while the floater or taskbox has focus.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- Type a command and run it with **`Ctrl + Enter`** or the Execute button.\r\n" L"- **`Ctrl + Space`** returns focus to the input field.\r\n" L"- **`Ctrl + Wheel`** changes the text size, **`Alt + Wheel`** the opacity, and a\r\n" L"  plain wheel scrolls.\r\n" L"- Long lines **wrap** instead of running off the right edge, so a transcript of\r\n" L"  window titles and help text reads without a horizontal scrollbar.\r\n" L"- The window keeps its own position, size, opacity, font, and font size in\r\n" L"  `config.ini`, independent of the other widgets.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Commands\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Every command that names a window uses the number `list` prints beside it, and\r\n" L"that is the window's place in the same list the toolbar draws. Only the commands\r\n" L"that print numbers re-read the window list: if `go` refreshed first, windows\r\n" L"could come and go between the list you read and the number you typed.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Command | Short | What it does |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `help` | `h` | The list below, inside the box. |\r\n" L"| `help move` | `h m` | One command explained in full, with examples. |\r\n" L"| `list` | `l` | Windows, numbered. |\r\n" L"| `list windows` | `l w` | The same list. |\r\n" L"| `list resize` | `l r` | The resize presets, numbered. |\r\n" L"| `list shortcut` | `l s` | The shortcut icons, numbered. |\r\n" L"| `go 1` | — | Focus window 1, restoring it if it is minimised. |\r\n" L"| `resize 1 1` | `r 1 1` | Resize window 1 to preset 1. |\r\n" L"| `move 1 100 100` | `m 1 100 100` | Move window 1 to 100, 100 on the display it is already on. |\r\n" L"| `move 1 100 100 2` | `m 1 100 100 2` | Move window 1 to 100, 100 on display 2. |\r\n" L"| `search windows word` | `s w word` | Windows whose title contains `word`, listed under their `list` numbers rather than renumbered. |\r\n" L"| `note` | — | Open the [note list](#10-notes). |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"`X` and `Y` are measured from the target display's own top-left corner, not from\r\n" L"the virtual desktop's, so the same pair of numbers means the same place on every\r\n" L"screen. The display number is the one the options menu shows beside that\r\n" L"monitor's name. A search term may contain spaces; everything after `windows` is\r\n" L"the term.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"`help` on its own prints one line per command; **`help <command>`** prints that\r\n" L"command's arguments, what they mean, and worked examples - `help move`,\r\n" L"`h search`, and so on.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"The input field is multi-line, so pasting several lines runs them in order, each\r\n" L"echoed behind a `>` prompt.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 9. Monitor Thumbnails\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**Preview Window** in a display's submenu opens a live thumbnail of that\r\n" L"display.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Click and drag inside a thumbnail** to drive the real monitor: mouse input\r\n" L"  is forwarded to it, so you can operate a screen you are not looking at.\r\n" L"- **Left drag the thumbnail's edit box** to move the thumbnail window.\r\n" L"- **Right click the edit box** to close it.\r\n" L"- Each thumbnail remembers its own position and size.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 10. Notes\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Open the note list with the **`N`** toolbar button or the `note` command.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Each note is a plain UTF-8 `.txt` file, so it stays readable and editable\r\n" L"outside hgfloater. **The first line is the title**; everything after it is the\r\n" L"body. Notes live in `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\note\\` and are named\r\n" L"`note-<id>-YYYYMMDD.txt`, where the date is the day the note was created.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**The list** starts with **`+Add Note`** and then shows one note per row:\r\n" L"creation date, last-modified date, and title.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"`+Add Note` is the row the list opens on, so `N` then `Enter` writes a new note\r\n" L"without a key to remember. It answers a single click, unlike the note rows, and\r\n" L"it is there even when there are no notes yet.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**Active and archived notes are separate.** Notes you are still writing stay on\r\n" L"top; archived ones settle underneath. Headings appear once something has been\r\n" L"archived - until then the list is just notes, and a label would be noise.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | What it does |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Enter` on `+Add Note`, or one click on it | Create a note and open it. |\r\n" L"| `Enter`, double click | Open the note in its own editor window. |\r\n" L"| `Insert` | Create a note and open it, from any row. |\r\n" L"| `K` | Archive the selected note, or restore it. |\r\n" L"| `Delete` | Delete the selected note. |\r\n" L"| `Esc` | Close the list. |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**Right-click a note** (or a heading) for that half of the list:\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Open**, **Archive** or **Restore**, and **Delete**.\r\n" L"- **Sort by** created, modified, or title, each ascending or descending, with\r\n" L"  the one in force ticked.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Any note can be deleted, archived or not: archiving files a note away, it does\r\n" L"not lock it. Deleted notes go to the **Recycle Bin**, so a mistaken `Delete` is\r\n" L"recoverable where Windows would normally recover it.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"The two halves sort independently: you can keep what you are writing by most\r\n" L"recently changed and your archive by title. Each half remembers its own order.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**The editors** are separate windows, and several can be open at once. Typing\r\n" L"updates the title as soon as the first line changes. `Esc` closes an editor.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**Right-click inside an editor** for the text and for the note itself: **Undo**,\r\n" L"**Redo**, **Cut**, **Copy**, **Paste**, **Delete** (the selection), **Select All**, then\r\n" L"**Archive** or **Restore**, and **Delete Note and Close**. Entries that would do\r\n" L"nothing are greyed, so the menu says what is possible rather than failing\r\n" L"quietly. It replaces the edit control's stock menu, which knows about the text\r\n" L"but nothing about the note it belongs to.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Undo goes back a hundred steps and **`Ctrl + Y`** or **Redo** comes forward\r\n" L"again, because the editor hosts a rich edit control rather than the plain one,\r\n" L"whose single undo level only toggles. If that control cannot be loaded the\r\n" L"editor still opens on the plain one, with Redo greyed out.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**`Ctrl + Wheel`** over the list or over any editor changes the note text size.\r\n" L"There is one size for all of them, so the list and every open editor follow the\r\n" L"same wheel. It is stored unscaled, so a change made on a 200% display reads the\r\n" L"same on a 100% one, and a note dragged between monitors is redrawn at that\r\n" L"display's scale. A plain wheel still scrolls.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"**Saving** happens on its own. Edits are held in memory and written a couple of\r\n" L"seconds after the typing settles, and only for the notes that actually changed,\r\n" L"so holding a key down does not turn into one write per character. Closing an\r\n" L"editor and quitting the app both flush whatever is still pending.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Everything a text file cannot carry lives in `note\\note.ini` beside the notes:\r\n" L"which half of the list each note sits in, how each half is sorted, the shared\r\n" L"text size, the size and position of the list window, and **where each note's own\r\n" L"editor window was last left** - so a note reopens on the monitor and at the size you left it. The\r\n" L"creation time of day goes there too, since the file name only carries the day.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 11. Keyboard Reference\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Global\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Win + Alt + Space` | Show or hide the taskbox (configurable). If a window has drifted off screen, it is pulled back into the nearest monitor's work area. |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Inside the taskbox\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Arrow keys` / `WASD` | Move focus between icons |\r\n" L"| `Space` | Activate the focused item |\r\n" L"| `Enter` / `F2` | Open the focused item's context menu |\r\n" L"| `C` | Open the Command Box |\r\n" L"| `Esc` | Hide the taskbox and re-scan shortcuts |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Inside the note list\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Enter` on `+Add Note` | Create a note and open it |\r\n" L"| `Enter` on a note | Open it in its own editor |\r\n" L"| `Insert` | Create a note, from any row |\r\n" L"| `K` | Archive the selected note, or restore it |\r\n" L"| `Delete` | Delete the selected note (to the Recycle Bin) |\r\n" L"| `Esc` | Close the list |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Inside a note editor\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl + Z` / `Ctrl + Y` | Undo / redo, a hundred levels deep |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl + Wheel` | Note text size, shared with the list and every other editor |\r\n" L"| `Esc` | Close the editor (pending edits are written first) |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### Window manipulation (focused or hovered floater/taskbox)\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `Alt` + `Arrow keys` / `WASD` | Move the window |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl` + `+` / `-` | Font and icon size |\r\n" L"| `Alt` + `+` / `-` | Opacity |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl` + `R` / `0`, `F5` | Reset position, size, and opacity |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### System\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Action |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `F1` | About |\r\n" L"| `T` | Open the taskbox (from the floater) |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl` + `Q` / `X`, `Alt + F4` | Quit |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl + Enter` | Execute (inside the Command Box) |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl + Space` | Focus the input (inside the Command Box) |\r\n" L"| `Ctrl + Wheel` | Text size (inside the Command Box) |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 12. Mouse Reference\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Action | Gesture |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| **Show / hide the taskbox** | Hover or left-click the floater, or press the global hotkey |\r\n" L"| **Activate an item** | Left-click an icon |\r\n" L"| **Reorder icons** | Left-drag a task icon |\r\n" L"| **Item context menu** | Right-click an icon |\r\n" L"| **Options menu** | Left-click `O`, or right-click the status line |\r\n" L"| **Move a window** | Left-drag empty space, the status line, or the `M` button |\r\n" L"| **Move the floater** | Left-click `F`, then drag the floater |\r\n" L"| **Move the taskbox aside** | Left-click the `M` button |\r\n" L"| **Resize the taskbox grid** | Drag a border, or drag the `R` button |\r\n" L"| **Font / icon size** | `Ctrl` + wheel |\r\n" L"| **Opacity** | `Alt` + wheel, or wheel over `A` |\r\n" L"| **Screen brightness** | Wheel over `B` |\r\n" L"| **Volume / mute** | Wheel over `V` / click `V` |\r\n" L"| **Pin the taskbox** | Left-click `P` |\r\n" L"| **Remote monitor control** | Click or drag inside a monitor thumbnail |\r\n" L"| **Open the notes** | Left-click `N` |\r\n" L"| **New note** | Left-click `+Add Note` (a single click, unlike a note row) |\r\n" L"| **Open a note** | Double-click it |\r\n" L"| **Sort, archive, or delete a note** | Right-click it, or right-click a section heading |\r\n" L"| **Note text size** | `Ctrl` + wheel over the list or an editor |\r\n" L"| **Edit a note's text** | Right-click inside an editor |\r\n" L"| **Command box text size** | `Ctrl` + wheel |\r\n" L"| **Quit** | Left-click `X`, or Exit in the options menu |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 13. Configuration File\r\n" L"\r\n" L"`%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\config.ini`, plain INI, safe to edit by hand\r\n" L"while the program is closed. Missing keys are written back with their defaults on\r\n" L"startup, so deleting a key restores it.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Settings that change in bursts — opacity, font and icon size, window position —\r\n" L"are written once the change settles (about a second) or at exit, rather than on\r\n" L"every wheel notch.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### `[floater]` and `[taskbox]`\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Meaning |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `x`, `y` | Top-left screen coordinates |\r\n" L"| `w`, `h` | Width and height |\r\n" L"| `alpha` | Opacity, 76–255 |\r\n" L"| `font_size` | Text size |\r\n" L"| `icon_size` | Icon resolution (`[taskbox]` only) |\r\n" L"| `show_stats` | `0` hides the CPU/memory/battery line (`[floater]` only, default `1`) |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### `[commandbox]`\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Its own `x`, `y`, `w`, `h`, `alpha`, `font_size`, and `font_name`.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### `[etc]`\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Meaning |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `font_name` | Font for edit controls, tooltips, and the About dialog (default `Segoe UI`) |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### `[colors]`\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Every accent color as `RRGGBB` hex, for example `FFD228`:\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- `scheme_bg`, `scheme_border`, `scheme_text`, `scheme_flash`, `scheme_selected`\r\n" L"  — the dark palette.\r\n" L"- `focus_bg` — the keyboard/mouse focus highlight.\r\n" L"- `stat_cpu`, `stat_temp`, `stat_gpu`, `stat_mem`, `stat_bat` — the floater's status bars.\r\n" L"- `value_alpha_low` / `value_alpha_high`, `value_brightness_low` /\r\n" L"  `value_brightness_high`, `value_volume_low` / `value_volume_high` — the\r\n" L"  gradients behind the `A`, `B`, and `V` buttons.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"### `[hotkeys]`\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Key | Meaning |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `global_focus_modifiers` | Modifier bitmask: `Alt=1`, `Ctrl=2`, `Shift=4`, `Win=8`. Default `9` (Win + Alt) |\r\n" L"| `global_focus_key` | Virtual key code of the trigger. Default `32` (Space) |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 14. Files and Directories\r\n" L"\r\n" L"| Path | Purpose |\r\n" L"| :--- | :--- |\r\n" L"| `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\` | Base directory, created on first run |\r\n" L"| `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\config.ini` | Every setting |\r\n" L"| `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\shortcuts\\` | Your `.lnk` and `.url` files |\r\n" L"| `%USERPROFILE%\\.HellGates\\hgfloater\\note\\` | One `.txt` per [note](#10-notes), plus `note.ini` for everything the text files cannot carry |\r\n" L"\r\n" L"That is the complete list on disk. The only thing written outside it is the\r\n" L"`Run` registry value behind [Start with Windows](#7-the-options-menu), and only\r\n" L"while that is switched on. hgfloater writes no log files, no caches, and no\r\n" L"temporary files, and nothing it writes grows without bound.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"\r\n" L"\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 18. About the Developer\r\n" L"\r\n" L"- **Author**: rubidus-api (rubidus@gmail.com)\r\n" L"- **Method**: developed with AI assistance, in the \"vibe coding\" style.\r\n" L"- **Note**: the author is a hobbyist, not a career programmer. This project is\r\n" L"  the result of creative experimentation and collaboration with AI tools.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 19. The HellGates Series\r\n" L"\r\n" L"\"HellGates\" is a playful parody of Bill Gates and Windows: a collection of\r\n" L"utilities meant to make the desktop lighter and more responsive. The series\r\n" L"started from wishing Windows were friendlier, quicker, and less in the way, and\r\n" L"it exists to try UX ideas that the stock shell will not.\r\n" L"\r\n" L"## 20. License\r\n" L"\r\n" L"Released under the MIT License. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\r\n" L"\r\n"
#endif